  "text": "        Jailbreaking my car's navigation system just so I can change the time | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Jailbreaking my car's navigation system just so I can change the time   (  nns.ee  )          393 points  by  xx_ns    5 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  145 comments                          help    \n      \n                    loufe    2 minutes ago       |  next   [–]       \n  I've been thinking about this for a 2016 Toyota in the family which has a quirky but decent enough idea for the main screen where a local DB file is consulted and a saved picture of the currently playing artist is displayed. Sounds great but there are some hilarious examples where the fuzzing of the name (best guess, I figure in case the name is slightly wrongly typed - mp3 era) is far too loose. \"Bayonne\" is showing a picture of Beyoncé and \"Steve Lacy\" is showing a bald white guy. It has become one of those things in life where I get a bit excited to see who a brand new artist will get as the matched image, reminds me of childhood, honestly. I figure with the game decompilation news I'm seeing the reverse engineering of the firmware (or at least the DB format) would not be a significant challenge, and it would mix in some fun other tasks like compiling (and signing?) a new DB with tons of images and names, and then figuring out how to avoid bricking a vehicles computer while doing so. But... like I assume many others here... I've got enough projects on the go right now so maybe in a few months.      reply                             numpad0    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I've heard that some Subaru models have 3 clocks in the view of the driver, all on the CAN bus, one being the nav and another being a remote display for the nav, and one that's just there, of which none sync to each others. I've looked up how it happened, and discovered that to just to add insult to injury, at least one uses a uC that has RTC feature but no XTAL pin for 32.768kHz or one of any sane base 2 divisions and uses CPU clock of 15MHz or something for it, so it just drifts away. I don't understand how Subaru stays in the business. Just how. It's a 4 digit time. Just broadcast it on CAN. Why not. Also: a lot of these older, completely rectangular sat nav units actually follow what is colloqially known as \"2DIN\" standards. (cheap import Amazon)Aftermarket units are available and those can be swapped in with just a bit of work. That's what most people do when they have issues with the stock unit installed for their markets.      reply                             spaceywilly    1 minute ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Most people do not go into a car dealership and say “show me your model that has the most accurate clocks”. Subaru stays in business because their models are basic reliable transportation, which is what people do ask for when they go to a car dealership.      reply                             giancarlostoro    9 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > I don't understand how Subaru stays in the business. Just how. Not sure if relevant, but Toyota has been buying parts of Subaru over the years, in 2020 ~ they have roughly 20% of Subaru. I'm not sure if its one of those \"Microsoft investing into Apple\" or \"Intel investing into AMD\" type of moments (which tbh, I wonder if Nvidia should be trying to invest in Intel or AMD since they're doing so freaking well its horrifying).      reply                             ManuelKiessling    35 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  My smart fortwo has two clocks, one on the radio, one in the driver’s dashboard; Both go wrong over time, but both in two different directions, so the trick is to calculate the „average“ between both, and you end up with roughly the correct time.      reply                             ErroneousBosh    15 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  My '97 Range Rover has an analogue clock with a quartz-controlled movement, and a button to set the time. It gains anything up to an hour a day, particularly when my six-year-old is helping me clean the car and he pushes the button to watch the hands go round really fast. It's fine. Time is time, we get there when we get there.      reply                             emeril    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  subaru infotainment is junk but they are a good deal for a safe car      reply                             unfocused    25 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I can confirm. My Subaru 2023 forester's infotainment unit got replaced after 1.5 years due to it randomly crashing, and Carplay being unstable. On a long road trip, if you go to get gas, unplug your phone, and try to replug, just wouldn't connect. Even rebooting it. Nothing.\nAlso had my 2 front speakers stop working, which mean handsfree didn't work since phone calls are routed to front speakers only. Had to unplug the car battery, wait, and then the speakers worked. Does anyone know why the mini display on the top, lags so much when changing music tracks or even Radio stations? It even sometimes doesn't update the radio station, and just mixes up data from 2 different stations. Replacing the infotainment unit didn't fix that or the lag. It's like purposely designed to be annoying.      reply                             tshaddox    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I've heard that the redesigned 2026 Outback has a much nicer interior. Lots of physical buttons and supposedly more stable infotainment software. We've been pretty happy with our 2020 Outback so far (bought it just before the chip shortage). My only complaint is the stability of the infotainment software. The big portrait screen is actually quite nice, and with CarPlay I don't really care about the rest of UI and software. Except that the stupid thing just  crashes all the time .      reply                             emeril    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  yeah, I've heard that too - I have a new forester - it's def slow/finicky maybe I'll get a outback instead next time, they are kind of the same to me though the hybrid on the forester I have def makes it a more pleasant less CVT'y ride...      reply                             tshaddox    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm hoping they bring the new Outback design (both exterior and interior) to the Ascent. The 2027 Ascent is already out and unfortunately it's the same old styling and infotainment.      reply                             MattSayar    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I also only use Android Auto, and plugging my phone in updates all the clocks automatically      reply                             john_strinlai    21 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  indeed, i have had several subarus in my life from a bone stock 98 impreza RS through to the newest STI, and while i  love  them and will probably never buy another brand, the infotainment (and speakers) absolutely suck in all of them.      reply                             loeg    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  And their engines have struggled with gasket issues and oil consumption for a long time.  People here love the AWD, though (my state enshrines AWD as a special class in law, artificially driving some demand).      reply                             clickety_clack    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That makes sense. AWD handling is so much better in winter conditions that I feel it has to make it less likely to crash.      reply                             numpad0    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Of the EJ20 just to keep it easy to play with the blocks without needing a modified type certs right? None of my business but they seem to be a bit unserious somewhat within bounds of a car company.      reply                             rogerrogerr    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  What benefits do you get from having an AWD car in your state? Tax treatment?      reply                             galleywest200    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  In western Washington State everything is on a hill and it feels as if the road is always wet all the time due to rain. Subaru is the most popular car brand in the whole state because it handles these conditions well.      reply                             hinkley    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Wet all the time isn’t a serious loss of traction. Wet after a dry spell is, and everyone there forgets it every time. You will have delays on the highways due to wrecks. Nobody has the slightest clue how to deal with ice.      reply                             zrm    28 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Wet  is  a significant loss of traction, it's just that wet after a dry spell is even worse. I'm from the Northeast but happened to be in Seattle on a day when it snowed. Every road was a hill and every hill was full of sideways cars.      reply                             stronglikedan    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Just the benefit of driving AWD is enough for me. It's hard to go back...      reply                             1_1xdev1    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  With AWD, the tire requirements when it’s snowing in the mountains are more relaxed compared to 2wd vehicles.      reply                             hinkley    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  However every time one of your tires goes bad you have to replace all four.      reply                             internetter    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is a niche problem but I live in Colorado      reply                             kridsdale1    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Mountains.      reply                             jerlam    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I had a luxury car from 2001 where the time could not be manually changed, only set to specific time zones. Of course, all the time zone rules had long diverged from its rule set, and it had to be changed four times a year to show the correct time. This car also inexplicably forced you to manually choose a country, and then a time zone, despite having a GPS that already knew exactly where you were and already had chosen a previous time zone. Carplay is nice but I would have settled for a non-luxury \"clock set\" button.      reply                             bobmcnamara    3 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  My 2025 Toyota has two clocks, one from Toyota, one from Android auto. The Toyota one requires selling your driving data to insurance companies.to update the tzoffset based on location. And so two clocks on the dashboard are off this week until I can remember where the dst adjustment is in the settings.      reply                             Uncle_Brumpus    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  My 2019 Subaru has a red 7-segment LED clock below the touchscreen that has 2 physical buttons right there that change the hour and minute. It's a luxury I don't think I'll soon take for granted. I changed my battery recently (the original OEM battery finally got too tired), so I've set my clock twice in the last week, taking about 10 seconds and 1/2 second to accomplish respectively.      reply                             onaclov2000    2 hours ago       |  root 