ted by the power company. So around 2am DST they would either send power at 59Hz* to fall back or 61Hz* to spring forward. This was from the era when power grids were separate and analog. With newer national grid-tie it might not even be possible to  do now.      reply                             Majromax    38 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > I was told by our school electrician that long ago the clocks were adjusted by the power company. So around 2am DST they would either send power at 59Hz* to fall back or 61Hz* to spring forward. This was from the era when power grids were separate and analog. That wouldn't have worked. Grid-tied synchronous clocks ( https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electric_clock ) were once ubiquitous and did derive their timekeeping stability from the grid frequency, but that is only suitable for  keeping  time once set.  It works because electric grids are frequency-controlled and stabilized, where deviations from the reference frequency are corrected over long intervals. In principle you could send power at 59 or 61Hz to adjust these clocks, but there are two problems: 1. The grid frequency isn't arbitrary, it's also  directly  coupled to the rotary inertia of every turbine that powers the grid.  This is why power fluctuations cause frequency changes: temporary oversupply or undersupply causes these turbines to slow down or speed up respectively, using their inertia as power storage.  Potentially screwing up every other piece of mechanical equipment tied to the grid for the sake of clock changes is... a poor optimization. 2. A one-Hertz difference isn't enough to change the clocks in a timely way.  To change time by an hour you need to gain or lose 3600 * 60 = 216k cycles, but a 1Hz change gains or loses one cycle per second.  It'd take about 2.5 days to complete a one-hour change via this mechanism.      reply                             skipkey    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I don’t know about that, but I was told by someone who worked at a power generation plant in the 1970s that they had a clock in the plant, that they would compare to the time broadcast on the radio frequency that carried the official time, and any time it diverged they would slightly adjust the frequency of the alternating current until it matched.      reply                             yardie    2 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I was a kid but I do know the clocks in my elementary, middle, and high school were accurate. Near end of day, we would impatiently stare at the clock waiting for the release bell. Being a public school, the clocks were bolted securely to the wall. Being little shits, students would probably steal them if they could, LOL. So how did they keep 100s of clocks on time? I asked and was told by the school maintenance that it was done through power.      reply                             jandrese    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I remember in my elementary school the clocks were all synchronized to a system somewhere, presumably in the head office.  The day after going back to school after a DST change the clocks would all be off by an hour and sometime in that morning they would all suddenly freeze, then fast forward or rewind an hour and then restart.  In retrospect it is amusing that whomever was in charge of updating the clocks didn't do it when they first got in but waited until the school day had started.      reply                             EvanAnderson    3 hours ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's crazy they didn't have a centralized clock system in a facility like that. What a thing to scrimp on.      reply                             jandrese    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  What are the odds they did have a centralized system for setting the clocks, but nobody knew how to operate it or cared enou