ch it to the product somewhere on the assembly line. The idea that software is the interface to the user, a signal of quality, part of product differentiation, an ecosystem in which future products live... none of this even remotely occurs to most hardware companies, especially car manufacturers.      reply                             numpad0    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Isn't it because the nav feature is market specific? People generally don't seem to like nav software brought in from different continents, across the planet.      reply                             lefra    27 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm european, recently went to Korea. The nav system was almost perfect, only complaint I have is that it signalled every single speed bump (I can see them, thanks). There probably was a setting somewhere to tell it not to, but I didn't care enough to try to find it.      reply                             yitchelle    51 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Apt time to share this video about jailbreaking a car's navigation system to get its PIN to unlock it.  https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oC6AiLqjtRg       reply                             js2    15 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Bravo, but: > When I bought the car, the previous owner asked me whether I wanted a \"navigation map SD card\" with it. Why would they even ask? > Since the maps on the SD card were apparently very out of date and didn't function properly, I decided I didn't need it and the previous owner tossed it. WTF? My 2016 Mazda has an out-of-date SD card too. I removed it because it slightly speeds up how fast the system boots without it, but it's in the glove box for any future owner when I sell the car. I just can't understand tossing it. It's not like it takes up any room. (My Mazda also sets its time via GPS, but I can get to the timezone offset and DST checkbox regardless of the presence of 