  "text": "        Postman silently auto-installs AI plugins and attempts to share all API data | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Postman silently auto-installs AI plugins and attempts to share all API data   (  github.com/postmanlabs  )          667 points  by  sidgarimella    2 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  217 comments                          help    \n      \n                    pants2    2 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  It's a shame what happened to Postman. It started as an excellent little developer tool for testing APIs. Then for some reason they raised over $400M in funding and hired 1,500+ employees, for what should have been a tiny OSS/passion project like curl or little snitch. Instead they now have to justify their valuation by forcing logins, collecting data, monthly subscriptions, AI features, etc. I still don't know how they're valued at $5B+ when I don't know a single developer who actually uses it anymore      reply                             swatcoder    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > for some reason they raised over $400M in funding and hired 1,500+ employees You know the reason. A bunch of people were able to take home a lot of money because of that growth and now have the option to FIRE themselves whenever they want to shake off the stress. That's the founder mentality that Y Combinator exists to leverage or encourage and it's ubiquity is what separates the prevailing 21st century technology movement from the nerdy, academic, UNIX-y movement that gave us GNU, linux, curl, ffmpeg, redis, etc We got to watch innovative products get pumped full of helium until they explode with bloat and rent-seeking instead of accumulating a growing foundation of sturdy technical building blocks as we could have had on the other path. AI now leads us into some new movement, the worst version of which a few model vendors protected by regulations absorb almost all technical accomplishment and lease it back out to everybody else, and the best version of which democratizes technical construction so widely that individuals can naively spin up effective solutions for the most idiosyncratic needs. We'll see how it goes.      reply                             mifydev    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I miss the UNIX-y movement so much :(      reply                             zaik    58 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It's not dead??      reply                             conductr    46 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It’s as dead as rock and roll      reply                             bigyabai    26 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  UNIX is, UNIX-y stuff isn't.      reply                             cnity    55 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  You are good at writing. Thanks for this.      reply                             godelmachine    40 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yes, this dude is really good at writing.      reply                             terribleperson    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I'm already enjoying the second version. I just hope we get to keep it.      reply                             vrganj    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   Hoarders can get piles of money, That is true, hackers, that is true. But they cannot help their neighbors; That's not good, hackers, that's not good. Join us now and share the software; You'll be free, hackers, you'll be free.  -- The Free Software Song, as written and sung by rms  https://www.gnu.org/music/free-software-song.en.html       reply                             netik    32 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  While I agree wit