 "page:https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50354336": {
  "v": 3,
  "fetched_at": "2026-11-03T20:11:47.476Z",
  "bytes": 10447,
  "text": "        Gitbook Breached | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Gitbook Breached          106 points  by  punchmesan    3 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  26 comments         We opened a ticket with support about Gitbook availability issues and got this in reply. We have yet to see any official customer communication or breach notification. Apologies for the formatting, did the best I could as it's a mobile paste. Update: GitBook security incident update\n Hey <$name>,\n The GitBook app, the GitBook API, and published docs are available again. A few services may still be degraded as we finish restoring everything to full performance. *What happened*\n GitBook has been the target of a sophisticated, highly targeted attack using AI agents. \n At 19:00 CET, November 2, the attacker gained unauthorized access to some of our infrastructure credentials. We detected the attack immediately and acted swiftly to block further impact. \n To make sure our infrastructure was fully secure, we then took the GitBook app and authenticated docs sites offline. We kept publicly published content live for as long as possible, using cached data. *What we did immediately*\n* Blocked the attacker and closed the vulnerabilities involved\n* Replaced every credential and secret across our infrastructure\n* Brought services back only once we were confident they were secure\n* Invalidated every customer’s API tokens on our platform *Your data*\n Our investigation shows that the attacker may have accessed the following:\n* Git Sync secrets.\n* GitBook API tokens.\n* Third-party API tokens used to connect external tools to GitBook.\n* Questions asked to GitBook Assistant by your site visitors. At this stage, we have found no evidence that any of your content in GitBook was modified. *What you need to do now*\n* Rotate any existing GitBook API tokens. We revoked all personal API tokens during the incident.\n* Rotate any third-party API tokens or secrets you use to connect other tools to GitBook.\n* If you have published integrations that use secrets, rotate those secrets.\n* If you use the GitBook MCP server, re-authenticate it.\n Please contact your GitBook organization’s admin to assist with this process. *What we are doing next*\n* Monitoring the situation in case of further attacks.\n* Introducing further security measures to our platform.\n* Writing a full post mortem that will be published soon with more detailed information about the incident.\n* If you have any further questions, please reach out to your account manager or our support team. You can also check status.gitbook.com for updates Thank you for your patience, and we apologize again for the disruption\n The GitBook team                 help    \n      \n                    SamyPesse    2 hours ago       |  next   [–]       \n  GitBook’s CTO here We have been dealing with a security attack since yesterday. Our team has been all hands on deck since the start, and we took a large portion of our services offline to prioritize user security. Our service is progressively coming back online (most of it already is), and we are actively monitoring the situation. We’ll continue to actively keep our users updated through emails, Slack, and our status page as we monitor the situation and learn more.      reply                             xmcp123    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Nice to see someone not fucking up outreach during a security incident. Good work.      reply                             john_strinlai    0 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > Nice to see someone not fucking up outreach  it is sort of funny to see this on a post submitted by a gitbook user that starts off by saying  \"We have yet to see any official customer communication or breach notification.\" , and where the company's status page ( https://www.gitbookstatus.com/incidents/01M6FXDDGQRWHMYYWXY2... ) doesn't mention the customer data that was potentially accessed by the attackers or mention that credentials/tokens should be rotated. in fact, the official status page still says  \"You don’t need to do anything right now.\"       reply                             throwrioawfo    2 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Did you read the body of this post?      reply                             gkoberger    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Good luck... this isn't easy, so best of luck to you and the team.      reply                             gk1    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Classy response. With the spate of attacks on infra providers over the past few weeks, it's been interesting to see how different competitors respond. The confidence some of the founders seem to have that they won't/can't be next is telling.      reply                             latchkey    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  After turning on 2FA, the page doesn't update to say it is turned on. It still has a configure button. Reloading the page fixes this.      reply                             MrZander    50 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > GitBook has been the target of a sophisticated, highly targeted attack using AI agents Serious question:  How do you know it was using AI agents?  What logs distinguish it from a human? I see this claim a lot and while I don't doubt AI is being used for this, I have a hard time believing every breach has a smoking gun showing it to be an AI assisted attack.      reply                             ipsum2    39 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  My guess is the speed of exfiltration/exploration of the environment is probably impossible for a human, or even a small team. But I'm also curious what their evidence is.      reply                             conartist6    41 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Maybe there was a level-of-stupidity tip-off, like trying to break in and access something that's already public...      reply                             FastDeep    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Good that they rotated all the tokens themselves instead of just telling users to do it, seen too many incidents where that step gets skipped.      reply                             petecooper    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   https://www.gitbook.com/status/security-incident-2026-11-03       reply                             eddythompson80    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Does a security incident page really need to connect to 11 3rd party domains. smh      reply                             philipwhiuk    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's bizarre they have both this and gitbookstatus.com and they contain different info.      reply                             SamyPesse    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  We summarized more information on this page from recent learnings, than what has been posted as updates on the status page ( https://www.gitbookstatus.com/incidents/01M6FXDDGQRWHMYYWXY2... ).      reply                             altbdoor    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Mentioned this 5h ago, but this post definitely fleshes out more details.  https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50351061       reply                             aliasxneo    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  \"GitBook has been the target of a sophisticated, highly targeted attack using AI agents.\" Add it to the list, I guess.      reply                             nsoam    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Is this what they mean when they talk about accountability laundering? Oh, you see, we had the best security ever, but they used AI agents, and there's no defence for that, you can't secure a system against those spooky AI agents.      reply                             SamyPesse    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  This is not what we meant. We will be accountable and will post a complete post-mortem. But it’s important to understand that the attack was a lot more developed than what we’ve been used to with humans, especially in terms of pace and adaptability.      reply                             punchmesan    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Oof that formatting is rougher than I thought. Will try to clean it up after I'm done reconfiguring gitbook...      reply                             verdverm    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  new lines between bullets, only italics available, not markdown      reply                             philipwhiuk    3 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  This is the public disclosure:  https://www.gitbookstatus.com/incidents/01M6FXDDGQRWHMYYWXY2...  Note that the summary at the top of  https://www.gitbookstatus.com  at time of writing does not mention the unauthorized access and the incident details does not mention the contents disclosed above.      reply                             latchkey    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  One thing that would be appreciated are more details in these sorts of reports. \"the attacker gained unauthorized access to some of our infrastructure credentials\" How? Which infrastructure?      reply                             SamyPesse    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  We’ll provide details in a long post-mortem. For now, it was important to notify our users as early as possible.      reply                          manytimesaway    2 hours ago       |  prev   [3 more]       \n [flagged]                              SamyPesse    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I’m genuinely sorry the end of friendco.de impacted you. It was 12-13 years ago; the service had little usage at the time, and we probably didn’t handle the end perfectly. I don’t recall exactly, but I can assure you that the team today is more experienced.      reply                             viccis    2 hours ago       |  parent  |  prev   [–]       \n  Not really relevant here.      reply             \n            \n   Guidelines  |  FAQ  |  Lists  |  API  |  Security  |  Legal  |  Apply to YC  |  Contact    \n Search:           "
 },
