  "text": "        Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too | Hacker News                  Hacker News   new  |  past  |  comments  |  ask  |  show  |  jobs  |  submit      login                           Qwen 3.8 27B on my laptop can interpret model weights, too   (  simonwillison.net  )          278 points  by  theanonymousone    2 hours ago      |  hide  |  past  |  favorite  |  83 comments                          help    \n      \n                    zkmon    20 minutes ago       |  next   [–]       \n  Qwen-3.8-27B - the best thing that happened in AI so far. This thing can accomplish the same results as the frontier models do, if you are willing to play a role of an higher level architect or decision maker. Guide it a bit here and there like a tech lead and you get everything done. The money you pay for frontier models is exactly for outsourcing that high level direction work to agents.      reply                             UncleOxidant    10 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > Qwen-3.8-27B - the best thing that happened in AI so far. Qwen3.8-Flash_Next should be in the running for that title as well. It's astonishingly good. I use both of them. If I need more memory on the machine for other things I run 27B, If I can get by with much less RAM I run QFN (takes about 102GB on my Strix Halo box with 262,144 context). QFN runs quite a bit faster on my hardware - about 50t/s. 27B with MTP starts out around 22t/s but degrades over time as the context fills up. Both are great models, but I'd give QFN a bit of an edge (at least for the coding projects I've worked on). But both feel on par with Claude Opus 4.6 - 4.8.      reply                             lnkdinsuxs    6 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > Guide it a bit here and there like a tech lead and you get everything done How do you validate and verify that outcome? Or does Qwen-3.8-27B do the validation and verification for you as well? If so, how do you ensure that's correct?      reply                             ActorNightly    0 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Gemma4:31b is better. Qwen is really good at speed (especially with megakernels), but if its going in a wrong direction, it will go in the wrong direction hard. Gemma can self correct. The only reason Qwen is more popular is because it can run decently fast on MLX, while Gemma needs a discrete GPU with 24gb of ram to run at usable speeds.      reply                             variaga    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Not making any claims about a specific model's capabilities, but all these \"can interpret model weights\" posts brings to mind the line in Shakespeare's Henry IV  GLENDOWE: I can call spirits from the vasty deep. HOTSPUR: Why, so can I, or so can any man, but will they come when you do call for them?  I can interpret weights too, but will that interpretation be correct?      reply                             layer8    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  They did test for correct interpretation, so what point are you making?      reply                             coldtea    10 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > I can interpret weights too, but will that interpretation be correct?  In TFA case, given that they were able to guess what the weights did (which the researchers knew in advance and could cross-check) and to even tweak and improve them, the answer is yes.      reply                             huijzer    31 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Biblically speaking, spirits from the vasty deep must be demons since they are from below and angels, God and Jesus are from above. So in that sense it’s not very impressive. Demons are something a sane person would rather avoid.      reply                             snapcaster    8 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Crazy how much the quote went over your head. Literature written for peasants 400 years ago and you can't grasp the point      reply                             giancarlostoro    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > I can interpret weights too, but will that interpretation be correct? Chances are much higher than a model can figure out what the patterns are, decipher them, and retain that knowledge in a meaningful way it can reason about. Me and you might take years or months to do all of that on our own, with a \"map\" of the weight we might be able to discuss it, but not as effortlessly as an LLM. Honestly, I had told a few different colleagues that reading a model is probably not that exclusive to frontier models. It's probably yet another reason OpenAI /  Anthropic don't release any big open weight models, but thankfully Google and Microsoft release reasonably sized models. Imagine a new GPT open weight release, it would get dissected drastically.      reply                             mwernolagoo    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Further than that, aren't \"weights\" just another sequence of tokens? We see them as vectors, pass to the API or as strings into a chatbox interface. Is it so surprising that the high-dimensionality linear algebra machine crunches them and gives us back useful vectors that can be mapped to text? Isn't that what this whole technology paradigm is about? How is it surprising? Granted, it is cool. But I see so much of \"look at this cool thing I did with LLMs\" and honestly... we need more structured text, more explicit documentation, more ergonomic and intuitive CRUD applications. The amount of \"look at cool things\" is growing way faster than before and they are all sort of low effort now because the \"agentic\" LLM-VM looping apparatus can do a lot of heavy lifting and run with our prompts. And I mean, if one has jargon, knowledge, and intention, they can also leverage that into great structured text, ergonomic and intuitive CRUD apps etc, all of which add great value to our mundane pursuits of applied research, software products and services and so on. Honestly, I begin to think the problem is people dazzling themselves instead of doing the housekeeping that is the bulk of life. Technology choice is entirely secondary.      reply                             D-Machine    52 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > Further than that, aren't \"weights\" just another sequence of tokens? No, not at all. I recommend you learn some of the basic linear algebra about how these things work before engaging in wild and completely nonsensical speculation.      reply                             mlyle    30 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I understand linear algebra.  It seems like he probably does, too. Are you sure you have read and understood his point? It seem quite possible that the flattened-model-weights ingested into a model have a much greater fidelity of representation in a similar model's KV cache than they do in a human's working memory, and that being able to loop and digest and chunk makes it unsurprising that models can excel at this type of task. I think that's the point the person is making.  Do you disagree?  If so, why?      reply                             D-Machine    11 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Weights are not sequences of tokens in any sense. This is pure nonsense. We don't \"see them\" [presumably, the weights] as vectors (they are matrices or tensors). There is nothing obvious or expected about weights being easily explainable or being enough to explain, because the weights are static: you need to look at the inputs, the activations, and the weights all together, you can never understand a model with the weights alone, unless it is a trivial model. The person isn't making any coherent points at all. There are also deep reasons to expect hard limits in intrepretability in general:  https://www.seangoedecke.com/alignment-is-impossible/#why-me... . EDIT: > It seem quite possible that the flattened-model-weights ingested into a model have a much greater fidelity of representation in a similar model's KV cache than they do in a human's working memory, Bringing up the KV cache is a sign you don't understand what you are talking about here. The KV cache is a computational efficiency thing and has no impact on how these models actually work (and isn't needed at all). The vague analogy to human working memory also just takes the cake here. > and that being able to loop and digest and chunk makes it unsurprising that models can excel at this type of task. This is how it does... all tasks.      reply                             lukan    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  And this reminds me of the king in the little prince, who had the power to order the sun to rise or go down. But only once a day at a certain time.      reply                             ChrisClark    25 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  But the whole point of the article was that it was correct. Why do this without testing? Why would you assume that?      reply                             athrowaway3z    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Hopefully we get a new benchmark: How well can a model tweak a set of weights to produce pelican on a bike?      reply                             Benjaminsen    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I actually build something to help with the whole pelicans on bike problem!  https://modelsalt.com       reply                             NitpickLawyer    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Simon, could you try reproducing Experiment 5 from the blog post, if you have a chance? Apparently this person tried [1] with API models and only the latest models can do the impressive 10-20x vs. PPO baseline. [1] -  https://nitter.netbub.com/AlexKrentsel/status/21173766837246...       reply                             layer8    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > I’m not ready to declare that the age of source code is over and our future involves incomprehensible machines editing their own model weights in incomprehensible ways. That sounds a bit like biology; maybe AI will become like that.      reply                             dotancohen    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Superior as mammals are to plants, so may AI become to us mammals.      reply                             lopsotronic    38 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Words like \"dominance\" and \"superior\" and such in this context are more interesting in how they're employed than in any inherent phenomenon they are intended to describe. Some poor-ass professor -- fighting tooth and nail for tenure, or at least some form of stability from all-powerful admin staff -- can't keep themselves from trying to figure out who's \"Dominant\" or \"Alpha\". When in reality the network isn't directed, but a mesh of arbitrary weights, vectors, and dimensionality. Perhaps if ancient China had beat us to Enlightenment and Industrial Revolution we would be seeing scientists talking about how respectful mammals are of their ancestors.      reply                             dotancohen    9 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That's paraphrased from Erewhon, which was written over 150 years ago and was the inspiration for Dune.      reply                             gavmor    54 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Not so sure mammals are \"superior\" to plants, nor fungi.      reply                             stuaxo    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Nope squishy is where it's at.      reply                             data-ottawa    38 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Perhaps I’m missing this, but can Qwen 3.8 alter Qwen 3.8 weights? There are a lot of known neurons in the older Qwen models that are long known to induce behaviour changes (see evil Qwen by changing one weight). My question boils down to how much is trained vs being able to actually understand the weights. Presumably a same gen model isn’t trained on any sources dissecting that generation. And how much do longer known conceits like steering vectors, J spaces, LoRAs play into this? This doesn’t explain the game training cases, which are very impressive to me.      reply                             seanmcdirmid    10 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  A Qwen3.8-27B model can probably do something with a Qwen3.5-0.8B model. I don't think this is so much the version (Qwen 3.8 vs Qwen 3.5) but the model size (27B vs 0.8B). But ya, having research on Qwen 3.5 definitely helps.      reply                             orsorna    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Why would you want to change weights directly versus running through a new training cycle? For example, I have collected a bunch of data I would like to train weights that I use local inference for. I figured a LoRa would be the way to go, perhaps be more cost effective and generalized too.      reply                             latentsea    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Because text-to-weights is a learnable function. Everything else seems to be. So, why not? Why train weights if you could just prompt for them. If we managed to train image edit models, then why can't we train model-edit models?      reply                             dghlsakjg    43 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Why would you do a whole new expensive, time-consuming training run if you can just tweak some numbers to get the result you want? I think your use case of incorporating novel data probably calls for training, but I'm sure there are use cases where you can tweak the weights to get what you want (say I wanted to tweak the model to be less sycophantic, or found a way to shrink it by removing weights associated with a language that I don't ever use)      reply                             bushido    48 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  In my latest experiments with editing the weights directly, it has edited the weights of my decision head and LoRa checkpoint. Essentially, my training introduced knowledge and structure which wasn't translating into results. I tried training in a few different ways, different ranks, different things, and I wasn't making any progress. With weight editing, a larger model was able to eliminate some undesirable pathways which the model was often using. Without going into the nitty-gritty, it basically took my best LoRa checkpoint and gave it the skills that I was unable to get with further training.      reply                             MisterMunchkin    59 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  If you could figure out exactly what certain weights do it would be way quicker to directly edit them, whereas training is non-deterministic and takes ages.      reply                             orsorna    55 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  And the modifications of those weights could be tracked in source control and overlayed on top of the base model? I see, there is a lot of potential in that.      reply                             elbear    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Because training is more expensive I guess      reply                             numpad0    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It would take a lot less FLOPS presumably      reply                             smrtinsert    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Speed, instant finetunes?  Highly optimized iteration cycles? The applications are the same for any reason why you might want local llms.  This is crazy exciting.      reply                             parineum    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Something someone else brought up in one of the previous threads was to identify the refusal routes and remove them.      reply                             xlayn    21 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  But that's obvious... Astra is Qwen, if Astra can is because Qwen can....      reply                             tarruda    2 hours ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder how far this can be taken. Could Qwen 3.8 can interpret and make improvements to Qwen 3.5 0.8B weights, or even its own weights?      reply                             f311a    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It can, as long as you can give it benchmarks. \nWhat it can't do is optimize general intelligence without any external validation. You can optimize a model by randomly changing its weights and validating results on your task. A monkey can type a book given enough attempts.      reply                             stymaar    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wouldn't be surprised at all if 3.8-27B could tweak 3.5-0.8B the same way Astra did, but it wouldn't be a big deal either because that part of the paper has nothing to do with directly tweaking the weights like the first part of the paper, but is instead a fairly straightforward abliteration method on a model that is known for a specific pathological behavior (ending up stuck in a thinking loop).      reply                             solnyshok    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  isn't consciousness just that, a thinking loop with interruptions for sleep until we break beyond repair? and, just maybe, if we managed to figure out the meaning of life, our thinking would satisfyingly stop?      reply                             latentsea    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  What I want to see someone try and succeed at would be to get the really highly quantized models to loop less. Many people with only 16GB VRAM are running the IQ3_XXS or IQ3_S quants of Qwen3.8-27B. If it could figure out how to improve those quants, that'd actually be pretty substantial. That's all they did for those improved math results is removed the looping. Why not? Please someone. Please.      reply                             rhdunn    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  It's fairly straightforward to write a function that detects looping output -- I wrote one for promptfoo in [1]. For training you would need to hook it up to a LoRA training harness, identify the looping weight direction, and adjust the weights accordingly. I've not (yet) looked into how to do this. [1]  https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=48555634       reply                             TMWNN    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n   https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/b/ba/DrawingHands....       reply                             awb    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Is RSI here? No.      reply                             m3kw9    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  i feel like this could turn out to be a over unity demo where they say the model can improve a model better than it's own. (more energy output than input)      reply                             boznz    39 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  All the talk is of the improvement in a capability, but has there been any study on What are these changes are breaking ?      reply                             boznz    34 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Only asking because I remember injecting bit errors in my local LLM back in 2024 with mixed and sometimes weird results so I definitely knew it was possible; finding out you can do this in a controlled way is frankly mind-blowing.      reply                             sagex    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Isn't the network too shallow to be challenging to decode? One can easily produce few layer network to solve specific goal with construction techniques, so not a surprising result in my view.      reply                             monkmartinez    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  What do you mean? Is there prior art? Would you call this a LORA? My first reaction to your question (rhetorical?) was uncharitable... so, I am curious what you meant that it was \"easy.\"      reply                             necovek    43 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I am surprised nobody caught on \"paper shaped\" for blog post: I chuckled!      reply                             simonw    34 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  That's an edit, I only added that about 15 minutes ago.      reply                             olalonde    17 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  What's funny about it?      reply                             wg0    37 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Off topic - anyone had the occasion to work with IBM Watson?      reply                             Towaway69    27 minutes ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Been too busy counting paperclips.      reply                             AlexCoventry    20 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I wonder if Qwen releases are deliberately nerfed for some reason. It's not the first time an easy reasoning performance win has been found for one.  https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.10947       reply                             gabrielcsapo    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  The question is can a 4b or smaller improve a 0.5b llm, or would it just get caught in a loop      reply                             devinprater    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Still amazing to me that a model that good, Qwen3.8:27b, runs on a laptop.      reply                             bsiverly    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Wasn’t this one of the main marketing points for the Inkling model as well?      reply                             tiborsaas    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  > It’s a cool paper, but some of the wider commentary I’ve seen around it does not feel justified. I’m not ready to declare that the age of source code is over and our future involves incomprehensible machines editing their own model weights in incomprehensible ways. The age of source code is definitely not going to end anytime soon if ever. But the future will involve incomprehensible machines editing other models in a \"fairly certain\" way, but we are going to need a lot of tooling to keep up with the process and not just take the model updates as \"trust me bro\".      reply                             badsectoracula    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  I did exactly the same thing with Qwen 3.8 27B but i also asked it something else: a couple years ago i wrote a simple upscaler[0] when i was learning about neural networks. The network is dead simple: it takes a 25 inputs for a 5x5 grid around a pixel and outputs the 2x2 pixel grid that the input pixel should be replaced for a (width 2)x(height 2) upscale. I trained it on a bunch of random images (in hindsight i should have trained it on game screenshots but w/e) and it looked quite nice. So i asked Qwen 3.8 27B two things: 1. What it is this network all about? I only gave it the ann.c code that implemented a simple artificial neural network library in C and the setup code that specified the network's shape but not its purpose. Qwen 3.8 27B tried a bunch of stuff, taking the layers one by one, proding numbers through it, etc and finally figured out it is some sort of 5x5 to 2x2 filter, most likely working with pixels (i never mentioned pixels or graphics), though it wasn't certain about the exact purpose. It did rule out a bunch of uses like being a downscaler or classifier, from its behavior. 2. After i revealed what it was, i asked it if it can improve it but keep it 100% compatible with the existing program that used the weights file. And sure enough, it did improve it. I gave it the original training C code and dataset. It made a few experiments (and wrote its own training C code because it found mine too slow :-P) and ended up improving the weights. The difference[1] is small, but it basically eliminated some small noise that was always there and made some transitions less \"halo-y\". Of course for the purpose i wanted it (use it to upscale games in realtime) it isn't that great (too heavy) but it was learning material anyway. Though one bonus thing i noticed is that it upscales my random doodles much better than any Krita filter[2] :-P. The above isn't Qwen 3.8 27B observing the weights directly and making sense out of them (ok actually it did seem to make some sense of the weights in the \"#1\" but i didn't dump the entire network to it - though thinking about it, it is only around 16384 floats so it might work). It just used a bunch of scripts it wrote in Python (and later C) but i still found it interesting to watch it go at it. [0]  https://mastodon.gamedev.place/@badsector/113765818432603610  [1]  http://runtimeterror.com/pages/iv/images/a3d94ea369ff95ecf05...  [2]  http://runtimeterror.com/pages/iv/images/4e29d027a8aa6e89db5...       reply                             dotancohen    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n       > the 2x2 pixel grid that the input pixel should be replaced for a (width2)x(height2) upscale.\n  \nWas it better than linear when repeating the process for 4x, 8x, and 16x upscale?      reply                             badsectoracula    45 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I hacked together a 4x (via applying it twice) scale once when i was modifying Gamescope to see how it'd look but it was both very slow and i didn't like the result so i only kept the 2x one[0]. Out of curiosity i did apply it twice to the drawing i posted in my previous comment with a 4x bilinear upscale made in Krita[1]. TBH i think a dedicated 4x network would do a better job though even then, there is only so much a network can hallucinate to fill in details :-P. [0]  https://github.com/badsectoracula/gamescope/tree/neural-netw...  [1]  http://runtimeterror.com/pages/iv/images/20b9558400bcf26511d...       reply                             simonw    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Which coding agent harness did you use with Qwen for that?      reply                             badsectoracula    59 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I've been using Pi though i'm currently doing the NIH thing and making a custom one in C++ with a web-based frontend :-P.      reply                             mccoyb    1 hour ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Why do people keep calling this a paper? It's a blog post which received feedback from collaborators. It hasn't gone through the academic paper lifecycle ... the authors themselves refer to it as a post and as preliminary. I feel like I'm taking crazy pills, but can we be precise when talking about work of this form? It started with referring to arXiv pre-prints as papers, and now we're at blog posts with an academic template as papers. A paper is a fucking paper: something which has been vetted by the scientific community, with at least some form (often, a few rounds) of anonymous review from people outside your circle of collaborators This is not there yet, and clearly so due to the various feedback from people (including the two pushback posts on HN) critiquing the lack of discussion about the existence of these techniques in mech interp, etc?      reply                             simonw    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Like you said, in the AI space a pre-print on arXiv is massively more likely than anything that's been through peer-review. I called it a paper here because it's by people with an academic affiliation and is of comparable quality (if not better than) many of the other \"papers\" we see. I also like it when a paper isn't a PDF. PDFs are a bad way of communicating information. (I will note that the authors themselves didn't call it a paper, their own BibTeX reference calls it a \"blog post\":)     @misc{takehi2026weightspace,\n    title        = {{Sparks of Weight-Space Programming: LLMs can natively read \\& write neural network weights}},\n    author       = {Takehi, Rikiya and Teoh, Jayden and Zhang, Alex and Khattab, Omar},\n    year         = {2026},\n    month        = nov,\n    howpublished = {\\url{https://rikiyat.github.io/blog/weight-space-programming/}},\n    note         = {Blog post}\n  }        reply                             cadamsdotcom    45 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  > PDFs are a bad way of communicating information. They have the strength of one PDF being frozen & (mostly) treated online as immutable - compared to a webpage which might change, or a blog post which might be edited. If a PDF needs to be replaced there'll be a notice. And the single-file nature of PDFs makes them more likely to sit in an archive not managed by the author (vs a blog) so they tend to live as long as the archive rather than the author's blog. For something that needs to Be referenced by others those are useful properties - I'd argue they outweigh how good PDF is at communicating information.      reply                             mccoyb    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Sure, I agree with point (3). But for (1, 2), just to point out, neither of your criteria would pass muster in the scientific communities who are ultimately responsible for vetting this work, right? The ideal is: we don't accept papers at NeurIPS (or wherever) because of affiliation, and the badge of \"quality\" is bestowed by peer review, from other scientists. > is of comparable quality (if not better than) But the posts that you refer to in your own post point out several ways in which the quality is not good ... But, in any case, I find it a bizarre choice to call it a paper when the authors themselves do not refer to it as such. We can agree to disagree, but just offering feedback.      reply                             simonw    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  You're right about this, but I don't regret my choice of words. I admit that I don't think formal\n\"papers\" deserve their elevated status in today's information economy. I think they add a whole lot of ceremony that usually results in a format that's harder to understand. So me calling this a \"paper\" was a little bit of a dig!      reply                             mccoyb    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Of course, I agree with you in many respects - but I’ve found the academic paper lifecycle to be tremendously helpful in terms of polishing my contributions (admittedly, not in ML - but in PL / systems) and avoiding content which could be construed as “misleading” You, being of sound mind, tried the technique and drew reasonable conclusions … but just look at the original post for this blog post: there are a large number of responses by people who clearly don’t understand the technique, missed the experimental weaknesses, couldn’t place the post into the lineage of mech interp results that preceded it … and will leave the discussion with the wrong impression. In your mind, whose fault is that? Is that that the unavoidable cost of a field, moving too rapidly to engage in peer review? In its ideal form, the paper lifecycle is not a gate, but a sharpening stone.      reply                             simonw    55 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  I think we have a messy version of peer review playing out right now. I'm not convinced the alternative - delaying publication by 6 months, keeping the new information available only to a much smaller group until then - would have been better. All of that said, I did just update my own post to include \"The paper (OK, the authors classify is as a “blog post”, but it’s paper-shaped) found that frontier models...\" inspired by this conversation.      reply                             mccoyb    47 minutes ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Agreed! Now if the authors update their post in response — we’ll have a proper venue on our hands!      reply                             MisterMunchkin    56 minutes ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  Because nobody actually reviews papers anyway so it is irrelevant. That might have happened when you only had 12 papers a year and they were things like “gravity exists”, but when you get a million papers a day on topics like “the ethnocentric illusion of west-canadian yarn spinning”, nobody has time for that.      reply                             DoesntMatter22    1 hour ago       |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It's not considered a paper until it's vetted by others?      reply                             mccoyb    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yes: before that stage, it is often called a \"pre-print\" ... if it is not at the state of a \"pre-print\", then \"blog post\" ... :)      reply                             davrosthedalek    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  In most fields of science, it's not a paper until it's accepted for publication in a journal. That normally includes peer review of some sort.      reply                             NitpickLawyer    1 hour ago       |  root  |  parent  |  next   [–]       \n  Yeah, that's not gonna happen in ML, where a positive result (even in blog form) can be (hopefully) trivially tested and confirmed. From memory, yolo and roberta and ppo were initially rejected. After a quick google search, pagerank was initially rejected, word2vec got one weak and one strong reject in reviews, LSTM got rejected (90s but still), dropout, knowledge distillation, and many more such cases. All of these are extremely important, foundational and field defining papers that ended up on arxiv or other preprint places. If that happened to those papers, there's 0 chance today that it'd be a good idea. The field is simply moving too fast, there's too much being published, and the \"top venues\" are simply swamped. Blog / preprint / carrier pigeon is ok atm, as long as someone can look at it, try it, and hopefully reproduce it.      reply                             alexcarterhq    48 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  have you tried it on wspbench?      reply                             0xbadcafebee    14 minutes ago       |  prev  |  next   [–]       \n  It literally just did math on some numbers based on a set of rules provided in the prompt in order to sketch a visual of a game. It didn't \"interpret model weights\" any more than an MP3 player \"interprets physics of electromagnetic waves\". The LLM built its own decoder on the fly for the games... which is what it's doing when you ask it to write code, calculate math, estimate the structural load bearing of a beam, etc.      reply                             m3kw9    1 hour ago       |  prev   [–]       \n  i don't get his example, he had a simple model that only plays pong and Qwen can tell by just looking at the weights?      reply                             simonw    47 minutes ago       |  parent   [–]       \n  You can see the full prompt which describes the challenge (and includes the model weights) for the Pong example here:  https://github.com/RikiyaT/lm_understand-logs/blob/main/exp1...       reply             \n            \n   Guidelines  |  FAQ  |  Lists  |  API  |  Security  |  Legal  |  Apply to Y